%N A400123 Number of strongly connected digraphs on n labeled nodes that are reducible as a flow graph rooted at every node.
%C A400123 Everywhere-reducibility is a local certificate: D qualifies iff for every node x, every strongly connected component of D - x receives edges from x targeting at most one of its nodes; such digraphs have at most 2(n-1) edges and are closed under edge reversal.
%C A400123 The certificate forces every block of the region decomposition to be single-entry and single-exit, so the exponential generating function closes as a species composition: Sum_n a(n) x^(n-1)/(n-1)! = Flat(C(x)) where Flat is the flat-skeleton e.g.f. (see flat_everywhere_reducible in the linked program) and C(x) = Sum_k C_k x^k/k! counts single-entry-single-exit all-valid blocks (see sese_all_valid_blocks in the linked program).
%C A400123 The block series is closed to all orders: C = x*Phi(C) where Phi is the e.g.f. of the flat-plus-one-return counts 1, 2, 19, 310, 7297, 225666, ... (see flat_plus_one_return in the linked program), which the return-attachment theorem expresses through the tree function tau = x*(2*exp(tau)-1)*(2*exp(tau)-1-tau) as Phi = F + tau*F*(2F-1)/(2F-1-2*tau*F), F = exp(tau).
%D A400123 M. S. Hecht and J. D. Ullman, Flow graph reducibility, SIAM J. Comput. 1 (1972), 188-202.
%D A400123 M. S. Hecht and J. D. Ullman, Characterizations of reducible flow graphs, J. ACM 21 (1974), 367-375.
%D A400123 R. W. Robinson, Counting digraphs with restrictions on the strong components, in T.-H. Ku (ed.), Combinatorics and Graph Theory '95, World Scientific, Singapore, 1995, pp. 343-354.
%D A400123 R. E. Tarjan, Testing flow graph reducibility, J. Comput. System Sci. 9 (1974), 355-365.
%H A400123 Gregory Morse, <a href="/A400123/a400123_1.py.txt">Python program</a>, Sep 2026.
%F A400123 E.g.f. (shifted): Sum_n a(n) x^(n-1)/(n-1)! = F(C(x)), C(x) = Sum_k C_k x^k/k!, C_k = 1, 4, 81, 2800, 137025, 8667936, 672150829, ...
%F A400123 C = x*Phi(C), Phi = exp(tau) + tau*exp(tau)*(2*exp(tau)-1)/(2*exp(tau)-1-2*tau*exp(tau)) with tau = x*(2*exp(tau)-1)*(2*exp(tau)-1-tau); F(x) = exp(tau(x)) is the e.g.f. of the flat skeletons.
